The message in your screenshot is:

Plain Text
UserWarning: X does not have valid feature names,
but DecisionTreeClassifier was fitted with feature names

This is a warning, not an error. Your model is likely still making predictions.

Why it happens

During training, you used:

Python
X = df[["attendance", "cgpa"]]

The model learned with column names:

Plain Text
attendance
cgpa

But in predict.py, you're probably doing:

Python
result = model.predict([[85, 8.2]])

This passes a plain list without column names, so scikit-learn shows a warning.

Best Fix

Update predict.py:

Python
import pandas as pd
import joblib
 
model = joblib.load("placement_model.pkl")
 
data = pd.DataFrame({
"attendance": [85],
"cgpa": [8.2]
})
 
result = model.predict(data)
 
print(result)

